NAME
       base64 - encode data as text, or decode it again

SYNOPSIS
       base64 [-w COLS] [FILE]
       base64 -d [-i] [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
       base64 turns any data (also binary data) into plain letters, digits,
       + and /, so it can travel safely through e-mail, JSON or a terminal.
       With -d it turns such text back into the original bytes. It reads FILE,
       or standard input when no file is given, and writes to standard output.

       Encoding is not encryption: anyone can decode the text.

OPTIONS
       -d, --decode
              Decode instead of encode.

       -i, --ignore-garbage
              When decoding, skip characters that are not part of the
              alphabet.

       -w COLS, --wrap=COLS
              Break encoded lines after COLS characters (76 by default);
              -w 0 writes one long line.

EXAMPLES
       Encode a word and decode it again:

           echo -n redhat | base64
           echo cmVkaGF0 | base64 -d

SEE ALSO
       od(1), xxd(1)
